From 9b1284128777cc2f0dfd593f6b81f5fa643276e1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: William Floyd <git@notmy.space>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 19:41:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 65/76] ipa: soft: agc: Keep highlights from clipping with
 dynamic range compression

With a mean luminance target alone, a scene with bright areas in it is
exposed for its mean and the bright areas clip. Vendor AECs adjust the
target from the histogram and, rather than darkening the whole image to
save the highlights, expose lower and let the tone mapping lift the rest
back up (Qualcomm's ADRC: its metering tuning gives a ceiling for the
brightest few percent of the pixels and a largest gain, around 2.4 in
daylight).

Add an optional histogram block to the Agc tuning, after the parameters
of Qualcomm's histogram luma target (its implementation is not public):
the mean level of the brightest pixels in a range (0-5%), scaled back up
by the DRC gain in effect, is compared with a ceiling. Above it, exposure
is held below the target by their ratio, up to maxDrcGain and filtered,
and the target itself moves down by at most the lower bound of
targetAdj; below it, a flat scene, the target rises by up to the upper
bound. The DRC gain is kept in the active state, and Adjust applies it
at the start of the tone curve, on the linear input, as
x * g / (1 + (g - 1) * x): shadows and mid-tones are scaled back up by
the gain, white stays white.

Signed-off-by: William Floyd <git@notmy.space>
